course: dictionary
lesson: 5183
title: Item: one thing in a list
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An item is one thing in a group, a list or a set of things. Look at the clues around the word. «A scale checks the weight of each item» tells you that an item is one separate thing, weighed on its own.
explain: In a shop, items are the things you buy. Shopping lists, to-do lists and packing lists are all made of items. To check your meaning, swap in the word "thing": "each thing" still makes sense, so item means one thing.
explain: In maths, items are often what we count, price or group. «A bracelet sold for £1 that cost 20p makes 80p» means the profit on one item is 80p. So 10 items make 10 × 80p = £8.
explain: Item also has a news meaning: a news item is one piece of news or a report. The word is the same, but the words around it tell you which meaning to use.
fact: An item is one thing, especially one thing in a list or a group.
fact: «Margin is the profit on each item» means the money you keep from selling one thing.
fact: A news item is one piece of news or a report in a newspaper, on the radio or online.
fact: Items can be counted, priced or grouped, as in household items or per item.
fact: Common phrases: an item of clothing, items on the list, per item.
fact: In lost property, each item should have your name on it, so it can be returned to you.
exercise: In «a scale checks the weight of each item», what is an item? | A barcode label | A group of people | A whole shop | *One single thing
exercise: «A bracelet sold for £1 that cost 20p makes 80p.» How much profit does one bracelet make? | £1 | *80p | £8 | 20p
exercise: What is a news item? | A type of food | A new kind of phone | *A piece of news or a report | A desk where newsreaders sit
test: Five items cost £2 each. What is the total cost? | *£10 | £3 | £2 | £7
test: Which word fits best? "Each ___ on the packing list must be ticked off." | whisper | ocean | *item | castle
exam: Why should each item in lost property have your name on it? | So it weighs more | *So it can be returned to its owner | So the shop can sell it again | So it looks neat on the shelf
